 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes are delivered to browsers over self-hosted WebSockets: the server holds a live connection to each browser and pushes new revisions as they commit. This meets the freshness target with low latency and keeps the board free of any realtime vendor, at the cost of owning connection capacity and reconnection handling. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together around this choice. The change store retains replayable revisions and publishes each committed revision reliably. Delivery manages the socket sessions and fans committed revisions out to connected browsers. Browsers hold a subscription and, after a drop, reconnect and catch up from the last revision they held.
 
